{
  "subtype": "what_if",
  "outcome_variable": "state_declined",
  "per_column": [
    {
      "name": "missing_source_id",
      "causal_role": "treatment",
      "named_in_query": false,
      "rationale": "Whether a submission is missing a source_id is a settable data-quality property of the submission; an actor could enforce that source_id is provided, making it an intervenable lever plausibly affecting decline."
    },
    {
      "name": "amount_bracket",
      "causal_role": "confounder",
      "named_in_query": false,
      "rationale": "Amount is largely determined by the underlying expense and drives decline risk; it is an intrinsic attribute rather than a lever one would set to reduce declines, and it can act as a common cause across submission types. Treated here as a covariate/confounder, not a true intervenable treatment."
    },
    {
      "name": "processing_lag_days",
      "causal_role": "collider",
      "named_in_query": false,
      "rationale": "Processing lag is measured relative to processing/decision events (values like -1 suggest it is defined post-outcome). It is downstream of or contemporaneous with the decision, so it behaves as a mediator/collider rather than a pre-treatment confounder."
    },
    {
      "name": "user_decline_rate",
      "causal_role": "other",
      "named_in_query": false,
      "rationale": "An aggregate of the outcome itself (historical decline rate per user). Being a function of the outcome, it is a leakage/proxy feature, not a clean confounder or intervenable treatment."
    },
    {
      "name": "category_decline_rate",
      "causal_role": "other",
      "named_in_query": false,
      "rationale": "Aggregated outcome rate by category; encodes the outcome and is not an intervenable lever nor a proper pre-treatment confounder."
    },
    {
      "name": "department_decline_rate",
      "causal_role": "other",
      "named_in_query": false,
      "rationale": "Aggregated outcome rate by department; a mechanical outcome-derived summary, not a confounder of a treatment nor a settable lever."
    }
  ],
  "treatment_present": true,
  "treatment_columns": ["missing_source_id"],
  "treatment_intervenable": true,
  "confounders": [
    {"name": "amount_bracket", "named_in_query": false, "plausibility": 0.4}
  ],
  "confounder_quality": 0.25,
  "rationale": "The query is explicitly interventional ('How can we reduce...'). Only missing_source_id qualifies as a genuinely intervenable treatment (a data-quality requirement an actor can enforce). However, the schema does not set up a proper causal design around it: the decline-rate columns (user/category/department) are aggregates of the OUTCOME itself, which are leakage-style features rather than confounders, and processing_lag_days is post-decision (mediator/collider). Only amount_bracket serves as a plausible, not-named-in-query confounder, and even it is a weak, generic covariate. No confounder was identified that is a common cause of the missing_source_id treatment specifically. The set therefore only weakly realises the what-if claim: a lever is present, but confounder adjustment for that lever is essentially absent."
}

